WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
2000 civilians in Oleshky have run out of food under Russian occupation. This is Omni View Top World News, Episode 5. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. Looking back over the week just ended, a few threads are worth catching up on. Washington threatened to quit the International Energy Agency unless it changed its climate focus. Argentina's poverty rate rose to 32.3%, which ended two years of decline. The United Nations added 61 companies

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.480
to a blacklist, linked to West Bank settlements.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.240
In Southern Ukraine, a woman waiting on the occupying forces for a way out wrote that she had

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.240
nothing left to eat.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.560
She said her weight had dropped from 110 kilograms to 67.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.780
Foreign Minister Andrii Sabiha told the UN General Assembly this is a humanitarian catastrophe,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.020
and he accused Russia of starving people and of standing as the only block on evacuation.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:01.060
Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov has called the situation critical, and he blamed Ukrainian

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.560
forces for stopping aid meant for those residents.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.860
Food was still getting in until May.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.220
Since then, the Guardian reports, Russian minefields and drones have cut the route, and

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.720
Ukrainian strikes on Russian supply lines have left frontline troops taking what civilians

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.380
had stored.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.300
The city once held 24,000 people.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.800
In South Africa, the BBC reports at least 27 people were killed in two mass shootings.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.700
The attacks were hours apart.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.620
They come while the country is trying to stop gang gun violence, and days after a house

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.680
parting near Durban left 11 people dead, including a pregnant woman.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.980
In Thailand, Bangkok City Administration has declared a disaster across the capital.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:47.260
The Guardian reports nearly 4,900 people are in shelters, and about 52,000 households

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:47.880
are affected.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.140
More than 330 millimeters of rain fell in some districts from Thursday through Sunday.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.340
Office worker Kosen Nilset, who was 44 and lives by the Prympra Chakorn Canal, said his

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.320
house sat underwater for about 30 hours, higher than the 2011 floods that killed more than

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.280
500 people.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.920
Governor Chad Chart CityPunt told people to skip trips they do not need.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:14.640
And he said about 1,000 people were sheltering in one school in Keha Ramklau.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.740
Canals were still high on Sunday morning, with crews trying to drain them.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.380
More rain was forecast for Sunday than a taper from Monday.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.000
In southwest England, police cleared homes on Sunday near RAF Fairford in Gloucestershire,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.360
a base American forces use, and arrested several men on suspicion of explosives offenses.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.900
France 24 reports the United States has used Fairford to launch operations against Iran.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.940
Police called it a major incident and said they believed it was contained.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.720
Army Bomb Disposal Specialists were checking a number of vehicles.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:53.820
In India, Maharashtra on Saturday declared drought in 265 of the state's 358 Talukas.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:02.480
The Hindu reports an 18.8% rainfall deficit, with 755.9 mm from June to the 18th of September.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:10.180
Yavat Mall has 16 of those Talukas, Jalgaon 15, and Ahilya Nagar, Nagpur, Amravati, and

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.520
Nanded 14 each.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.140
The order switches on 12 immediate reliefs, including a land revenue waiver, restructuring

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:22.500
of crop loans, a pause on farm loan recovery, and drinking water tankers, after a dry spell

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.780
of at least 21 days.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:30.060
On the 22nd of September, Chief Minister DeVendra Fadnovis set up a cabinet subcommittee under

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.300
Revenue Minister Chandra Shaker-Bowangkule.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:38.020
Deputy Chief Minister Sunetra Ajit Powar said help for crop losses would come in a later

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:38.920
government resolution.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.820
In the occupied West Bank, Finance Minister Bezilell Smotrich told Wynett that Israel

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.560
should go to war there and repeat what it did in Gaza.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:54.500
In Gaza, CBC puts the death toll above 74,000, and Israel's military says continued strikes

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.380
were responses to threats or attacks.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.680
In Brazil, dozen roll of 3.0 and two other measures may cost 8.2 billion dollars, mostly

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.540
not in the 2027 budget bill.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:10.580
In Northern Ireland, an appeals court allowed an orange order march down Portedown's

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.520
Garvagi Road for the first time in 28 years.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:20.700
Al Jazeera reports the U.S.-led Board of Peace has given UNRWA no role in Gaza, echoing

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.520
a long-standing Israeli demand.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.200
In Cape Town, the case on both sides is whether two Chakma Baboon troops should be rounded

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.140
up, sterilized, and kept in a new sanctuary or left to roam.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:37.200
The council says the removal is imminent, and its reason is the daily clash with people.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:43.440
Justin O'Reane, a behavioral ecologist at the University of Cape Town, says a house is hard to sell

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.140
when it is covered in baboon feces and poprins, and the animals return every day.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.660
Troops pull off roof sheets, slip into unlocked cars and empty pantries.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.560
Some stay out late, break into homes in Simon's Town, and take snacks from shops.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:02.660
Jocelyn Moore Mile, who leads the Cape Baboon Partnership, says the animals are drawn

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:07.320
to low ground for water and richer plants, but the peninsula has built over most of that

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:11.080
land, pinning them between Table Mountain National Park and the Atlantic.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:19.660
A 2025 census counted 493 baboons and 12 managed troops, up by a third from 360 in 2000,

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.420
after the city stopped calling in the late 1990s under public pressure. Animal rights activists

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:30.720
say the animal should stay free, that living alongside them is the workable path, and that milder

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.600
options have not been properly weighed. Both sides accept that two troops are breaking into homes

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.120
and shops. The council says the roundup is close. A sign of progress came on a football pitch.

